    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my thesis regarding the Maillard reaction in meat proteins and I'm a bit stuck on the specific interaction between amino acids and reducing sugars. Could you explain the molecular mechanism of how these two components initiate the reaction, and specifically how the concentration of reducing sugars affects the rate of non-enzymatic browning?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Maillard reaction initiates when the amino group of an amino acid reacts with the aldehyde or ketone group of a reducing sugar. This cyclization forms a glycosylamine intermediate, which rapidly undergoes Amadori rearrangement to form a key ketoamine. Increasing the concentration of reducing sugars directly increases the frequency of these initial collisions, thus accelerating the rate at which the non-enzymatic browning proceeds. Therefore, higher sugar concentrations generally lead to faster and more intense browning reactions in meat proteins.",
      "source": "target",
